From: Proton CRM <noreply@proton.local>
Subject: [PATCH] error messages an operator can act on, not raw HTTP bodies

Every Proton request helper threw `new Error(`${status}: ${text.slice(0,200)}`)`
and every admin page rendered that string directly. When a backend path is not
routed by the tenant's Caddy vhost the request falls through to Chatwoot, which
answers with its own HTML error page, so the alert preferences page showed an
operator:

    Could not load alert preferences: 404: <!DOCTYPE html> <html lang="en"> …

That is the worst possible message for that failure. The backend route was
mounted and healthy; the fault was a missing /alerts/* line in the reverse
proxy, and nothing in the UI could lead anyone there. It went unnoticed until
somebody clicked the page.

A new helper, `helper/protonError.js`, turns a failed response into a sentence:

  - An HTML body means the request never reached the backend at all -- now said
    in those words, with what an operator must fix. This is the case the old
    message hid completely.
  - FastAPI's own `detail` (or `reason`/`message`) is used when present, so a
    router that explains itself keeps its explanation.
  - 401/403/404/422/5xx each get a plain sentence naming the next step, e.g.
    403 points at Settings -> Roles & Permissions rather than saying "403".

Six throw sites across four files now call it. `.status` is preserved, because
callers branch on it (patch 0033's 404-means-not-configured path), and the raw
body moves to `.detail` -- still available to a console log or a test, never
rendered.

The hunks below are a real `git diff` against the four files as extracted from
the patched upstream image, no hand-computed @@ counts. `node --check` passes
on all five files.
